A fuzzy metric space is a triple $(X, M, *)$ where $X$ is a nonempty set, $*$ is a continuous t-norm on $[0,1]$ (i.e. a continuous, associative, commutative, nondecreasing binary operation on $[0,1]$ with identity $1$), and $M : X \times X \times (0,\infty) \to [0,1]$ satisfies, for all $x,y,z \in X$ and all $t,s > 0$:
(i) $M(x,y,t) > 0$;
(ii) $M(x,y,t) = 1$ for all $t > 0$ if and only if $x = y$;
(iii) $M(x,y,t) = M(y,x,t)$;
(iv) $M(x,y,t) * M(y,z,s) \le M(x,z,t+s)$;
(v) the map $t \mapsto M(x,y,t)$ from $(0,\infty)$ to $[0,1]$ is continuous.

A fuzzy metric space is called compact if its induced topology is compact. Two fuzzy metric spaces are isometric if there is a bijection between them preserving the fuzzy metric in all time variables.

Let $\mathrm{CFM}$ denote the set of isometry classes of compact fuzzy metric spaces, equipped with the fuzzy Gromov–Hausdorff metric (defined analogously to the classical Gromov–Hausdorff distance, via infima of Hausdorff-type fuzzy distances over all isometric embeddings of two representatives into a common ambient fuzzy metric space).

Question: Is $(\mathrm{CFM}, d_{\mathrm{GH}}^{\mathrm{fuzzy}})$ a separable fuzzy metric space, i.e. does it contain a countable dense subset?

